e  wooden structures and that's also the  why I am laying it out like this so we  kind of have this hill that fills in the  gap between these two hills that maybe  after that we could have a few more  Hills and it basically you get this sort  of skyline and if you're walking around  the paths or save this with a car park  you would see this amazing looking  skyline of a wooden roller coaster  layouts and that's really what we're  looking for here you generally don't  want to make it too flat or too compact  and really show off all the curves and  the hills of the coaster as much as you  can especially the transitions so  whenever you go from a hill into a curve  or from a curve into another curve you  want to smooth those things as much as  you can because these things can be  quite rough and I think that looks quite  good speed on the entire thing is pretty  good as well you don't want it to slow  down too much on these hills so I'm  gonna go down there and maybe what I  think we could do is I'm starting to go  and try to weigh my options here we  could actually have another hill over  here but so far we already have a lot of  hills in this coaster so what I'm  thinking is that we can actually add  another typical wooden rollercoaster  elements I think this would be a pretty  cool place for a double-up yeah  basically double ups and double downs  are those kind of elements that you see  a lot of wooden roller coasters that are  quite a lot of fun  and something that you can always add to  a wooden roller coaster whenever there's  a bit of space where you're not really  sure what else to do or there's a very  large section where you only want to  have one hill and it basically does what  the name says you go up twice or you go  down twice so in this case I'm going to  add this little here over here and then  I'm going to add another hill and  that'll be pretty much the top where  it's gonna crest over now again I want  to smooth this out just enough so that  this  doesn't become one really curve I think  they'll be fine  could be banked a little bit more it's  also another thing to keep in mind is  that the smoothing tool if you select  very large sections it is going to get  rid of some of your banking so usually I  sort of read Bank it as I'm going  through it with this smoothing tool  there we go and smooth out this part of  the track if you're building double ups  and double downs though always be very  careful with the smoothing tool because  if you use it too much you're just going  to eventually remove the entire double  up or double down element in its energy  is going to be one straight section of  track which is pretty boring and not  what we're looking for here now we're  already getting into the second part of  the coaster and we've already lost half  of our speed but we do want to get the  feeling that the coaster doesn't really  lose any speed throughout the entire  layout we want to keep that momentum so  I think this last part of the coaster I  want to mostly focus on small twists and  turns and Hills instead of the large  winding hills that we have in the first  half so one of the first things that I'm  going to try in the second part is see  if we can get this quick curve in over  here just make that a little bit larger  thing just to smooth it out a bit more  these moving to will eventually take  care of that and make it look a bit  better than it currently does and after  that I'm looking into possibly making a  over banked curve over the lifter we  don't have an over-banked curve yet it's  basically one curve where you go up and  you're going to go straight at the top  of the hill and have a very steep Bank  so basically something like this and  then after this you go down again and  that is really where the very basic  over-banked curve looks like it's a  great way to curve around if you want to  have a hill in the mean time as well and  in this case especially I'm trying to  find a way around the lift over here and  this does so perfectly now this is also  the time that I usually go to the back  end of the station and add a straight  but very slightly inclined piece of  track at the end of it which is the  space for the break run would you need  to actually slow down the coast before  it enters the station or in some cases  to have a transfer track where you can  store the second train of the coaster so  with that out of the way we could pretty  much finish the layout of this wooden  coaster with a very low and twisty part  that makes its way back to the end of  that straight piece of track which you  could also curve a bit into a different  direction than the station I should say  you just want to make sure that you have  that straight break run just before you  enter the station also a little piece of  advice  most wooden coasters try to get as many  head choppers as they can which are  these moments when the supports or the  track above the track which you're  riding on are so close that you almost  feel like you're gonna have your head  hit by some wooden supports it never  really happens because obviously you  want it to be safe but these moments  kind of make the coaster seem faster and  more exciting a bit like where the  coaster goes underneath the supports  over here or where the coaster really  goes into the support structure at this  spot here I also put a Double Down  element over here so you can actually  fly out of your seat quite a bit more at  this ending element and make it seem a  little bit faster than it really is  because at this point the coaster is  pretty slow and I'm trying to make my  way back into the station without making  it seem as if the coaster is very slow  so that also means a lot of curves that  are very very close to the ground here  and switch it up quite a bit until we  get into the final helix that brings us  back into the brake run now with one  more curve at the end and a very small  hill this is actually the finished  layout of the coaster and what I haven't  really mentioned yet I will go over the  POV and the way it looks in a minute is  that this is really a sort of classical  out and back kind of wooden roller  coaster where you go out there all the  way to the other side of the map almost  turn around and have all of these  different hills and twists all the way  back to the station which is the way  that you see many of these especially  the traditional wooden roller coasters  where you really go somewhere else  almost explore like the parking lots or  explore the sides of the park which is  why you usually see these kinds of  courses as well now you do see some of  the more compact wooden roller coasters  that really twist in and out of  themselves which do more or less the  same thing as well just have love curves  overbank turns  double ups and downs small  time heals except instead of being very  spread out and thin in how they lay out  is laid out there much more twisting in  and out of themselves you can do both  things but in this case I really wanted  to go with a more traditional looking  wooden roller coaster and I'm actually  quite happy with how the skyline turn  now it's sort of having the hills in the  back fill in the gaps in the hills of  the fronts and with that over-banked  curve kind of nicely ending the skyline  of the coaster now you guys won't have a  POV of course so I'm going to very  quickly jump into the front seats and  I'm not going to be that kind of person  who is going to say we or anything else  on the coaster sure I'll do that in real  life but at this point I kind of want to  comment again on the way that different  parts of the coaster work so at this  point you're really pressed into the  cosa whereas on the top of that hill  just now you're really gonna fly into  the air out of your seats since it has  just enough time to glide over the hill  like that and the same thing goes over  here you just want the tops of those  hills to fly over it w